1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:03,818
Every property in this course starts at the atom.

2
00:00:03,818 --> 00:00:08,398
Strength, hardness and conductivity all depend on how atoms are connected.

3
00:00:08,398 --> 00:00:12,386
So let us rebuild the atom, the way your chemistry review does.

4
00:00:13,106 --> 00:00:16,967
The Bohr model, from nineteen thirteen: a nucleus of protons

5
00:00:16,967 --> 00:00:20,828
and neutrons, with electrons orbiting in discrete shells.

6
00:00:21,248 --> 00:00:24,634
Z is the atomic number, the number of protons.

7
00:00:24,634 --> 00:00:29,386
N is the number of neutrons, and the atomic mass A is about Z plus N.

8
00:00:29,806 --> 00:00:32,802
Isotopes have the same Z but a different N.

9
00:00:32,802 --> 00:00:37,296
That is why carbon weighs twelve point zero one one, not twelve.

10
00:00:37,296 --> 00:00:40,411
It is the average of ninety eight point nine percent

11
00:00:40,411 --> 00:00:44,186
carbon twelve and one point one percent carbon thirteen.

12
00:00:44,606 --> 00:00:49,476
The later wave mechanical model replaces orbits with probability clouds.

13
00:00:49,476 --> 00:00:52,989
And electrons fill the lowest energy states first.

14
00:00:52,989 --> 00:00:56,133
Atoms with completely filled s and p subshells,

15
00:00:56,133 --> 00:00:59,462
the inert gases, are stable and unreactive.

16
00:01:03,162 --> 00:01:05,396
Your lecture gives a useful tip.

17
00:01:05,396 --> 00:01:10,608
Most of these problems are dimensional analysis with three numbers: density, atomic

18
00:01:10,608 --> 00:01:16,278
weight, and Avogadro's number, six point zero two two times ten to the twenty three.

19
00:01:16,698 --> 00:01:19,802
How many atoms are in six grams of carbon?

20
00:01:19,802 --> 00:01:22,715
Six grams over twelve point zero one one grams

21
00:01:22,715 --> 00:01:25,945
per mole is zero point four nine nine moles.

22
00:01:25,945 --> 00:01:31,836
Times Avogadro's number: three point zero one times ten to the twenty three atoms.

23
00:01:32,257 --> 00:01:34,725
And the volume of one mole of gold?

24
00:01:34,725 --> 00:01:38,485
Its atomic weight, one ninety six point nine seven grams per

25
00:01:38,485 --> 00:01:42,363
mole, over its density, nineteen point three two grams per

26
00:01:42,363 --> 00:01:46,123
cubic centimeter, is ten point two cubic centimeters.

27
00:01:49,922 --> 00:01:53,218
Which bond forms depends on electronegativity: an

28
00:01:53,218 --> 00:01:56,638
atom's power to attract the electrons in a bond.

29
00:01:56,638 --> 00:02:01,302
Pauling's scale runs from about zero point seven to four point zero.

30
00:02:01,722 --> 00:02:06,904
It increases from left to right across the periodic table, and from bottom to top.

31
00:02:06,904 --> 00:02:10,024
Elements on the left, like sodium and potassium,

32
00:02:10,024 --> 00:02:13,089
are electropositive: they give up electrons.

33
00:02:13,089 --> 00:02:15,540
Elements on the right, like fluorine and

34
00:02:15,540 --> 00:02:18,939
chlorine, are electronegative: they grab them.

35
00:02:19,359 --> 00:02:24,078
The difference in electronegativity between two atoms decides the type of bond.

36
00:02:27,878 --> 00:02:32,416
First, the bond itself. Bring two atoms together, and two forces act on

37
00:02:32,416 --> 00:02:35,530
them: an attractive force and a repulsive force.

38
00:02:35,950 --> 00:02:37,735
The net force is their sum.

39
00:02:37,735 --> 00:02:42,721
Where the net force is zero, the atoms sit at their equilibrium spacing, r zero.

40
00:02:43,141 --> 00:02:45,813
The energy is the integral of the force.

41
00:02:45,813 --> 00:02:50,248
Its minimum, the bottom of the well, is the bonding energy E zero:

42
00:02:50,248 --> 00:02:53,887
the energy needed to pull the two atoms completely apart.

43
00:02:54,307 --> 00:02:57,334
A deep well means a strongly bonded material.

44
00:02:57,334 --> 00:03:00,070
A shallow well, a weakly bonded one.

45
00:03:00,070 --> 00:03:03,155
And bond energy sets the state: strong bonds give

46
00:03:03,155 --> 00:03:07,113
solids, moderate bonds liquids, and weak bonds gases.

47
00:03:10,913 --> 00:03:14,344
There are three primary bonds. First, ionic.

48
00:03:14,344 --> 00:03:18,993
In sodium chloride, sodium gives its single valence electron to chlorine.

49
00:03:19,413 --> 00:03:24,621
Sodium becomes a positive ion and chlorine a negative ion, and they attract.

50
00:03:24,621 --> 00:03:28,871
Ionic bonding needs a large electronegativity difference, more than

51
00:03:28,871 --> 00:03:32,941
about one point seven, typically a metal with a nonmetal.

52
00:03:33,361 --> 00:03:37,826
Bond energies are six hundred to fifteen hundred kilojoules per mole.

53
00:03:37,826 --> 00:03:43,465
So ionic materials melt at high temperatures, are hard and brittle, and insulate.

54
00:03:43,465 --> 00:03:45,874
This is the main bond in ceramics.

55
00:03:46,594 --> 00:03:50,747
Second, covalent. Atoms with similar electronegativities share

56
00:03:50,747 --> 00:03:53,934
electrons, so each completes its outer shell.

57
00:03:53,934 --> 00:03:58,144
In methane, carbon shares one pair with each of four hydrogens.

58
00:03:58,564 --> 00:04:03,273
Covalent bonds are directional, so they fix the orientation of the atoms.

59
00:04:03,273 --> 00:04:05,291
Their properties vary widely.

60
00:04:05,291 --> 00:04:08,823
Diamond melts above three thousand five hundred degrees.

61
00:04:08,823 --> 00:04:11,290
Bismuth melts at two hundred seventy.

62
00:04:11,709 --> 00:04:16,459
Third, metallic. Each atom gives up its valence electrons to a sea of

63
00:04:16,459 --> 00:04:20,200
free electrons, drifting between positive ion cores.

64
00:04:20,621 --> 00:04:25,279
Free electrons give metals their high electrical and thermal conductivity.

65
00:04:25,279 --> 00:04:30,109
And because the bond is not directional, atoms can slide past each other.

66
00:04:30,109 --> 00:04:31,546
That is ductility.

67
00:04:35,345 --> 00:04:38,053
Most materials are neither one hundred percent

68
00:04:38,053 --> 00:04:40,761
ionic nor one hundred percent covalent.

69
00:04:40,761 --> 00:04:43,940
Your lecture gives the percent ionic character.

70
00:04:44,360 --> 00:04:50,658
For titanium dioxide, X titanium is one point five and X oxygen is three point five.

71
00:04:50,658 --> 00:04:54,370
The difference is two, so the exponent is minus zero

72
00:04:54,370 --> 00:04:58,148
point two five times four, which is minus one.

73
00:04:58,148 --> 00:05:02,855
One minus e to the minus one is about sixty three percent ionic.

74
00:05:03,275 --> 00:05:07,082
For zinc telluride, one point six and two point one.

75
00:05:07,082 --> 00:05:11,554
A difference of only zero point five gives about six percent ionic.

76
00:05:11,554 --> 00:05:13,729
That bond is mostly covalent.

77
00:05:17,529 --> 00:05:22,258
Secondary bonds, or van der Waals bonds, are physical, not chemical.

78
00:05:22,258 --> 00:05:25,084
They are about ten kilojoules per mole, compared

79
00:05:25,084 --> 00:05:27,802
with fifty to a thousand for primary bonds.

80
00:05:28,222 --> 00:05:29,950
They come from dipoles.

81
00:05:29,950 --> 00:05:34,384
A thermal vibration distorts an atom's electron cloud for an instant, and

82
00:05:34,384 --> 00:05:38,761
that dipole induces a dipole in its neighbor, so the two attract.

83
00:05:39,181 --> 00:05:42,072
Hydrogen bonding is the strongest kind.

84
00:05:42,072 --> 00:05:47,477
The bare proton in an O H, F H or N H bond attracts a neighboring molecule.

85
00:05:47,477 --> 00:05:50,682
That is what holds water molecules together.

86
00:05:54,382 --> 00:05:57,796
Now the payoff. Bond energy predicts properties.

87
00:05:57,796 --> 00:06:02,312
A deeper well, a larger E zero, means a higher melting temperature.

88
00:06:02,732 --> 00:06:08,328
A deeper, more sharply curved well means a stiffer bond, a higher elastic modulus.

89
00:06:08,328 --> 00:06:13,635
And it means less expansion on heating, a lower coefficient of thermal expansion.

90
00:06:14,055 --> 00:06:17,746
A quick check from your lecture. Brass is metallic.

91
00:06:17,746 --> 00:06:20,302
Rubber is covalent with van der Waals.

92
00:06:20,302 --> 00:06:22,630
Solid xenon is van der Waals only.

93
00:06:22,630 --> 00:06:25,413
And aluminum phosphide is mostly covalent.

94
00:06:29,313 --> 00:06:31,176
Here is the summary of topic two.

95
00:06:31,596 --> 00:06:35,314
The atom: Z protons, and a mass of about Z plus N.

96
00:06:35,733 --> 00:06:38,540
The electronegativity difference decides the bond.

97
00:06:38,960 --> 00:06:41,706
Ionic for big differences, covalent for shared

98
00:06:41,706 --> 00:06:44,562
electrons, metallic for the electron sea.

99
00:06:44,983 --> 00:06:47,232
And a deeper well means a higher melting

100
00:06:47,232 --> 00:06:50,605
point, a higher modulus, and lower expansion.

101
00:06:50,605 --> 00:06:53,641
Next, we arrange these atoms into crystals.
